(; constants used by Mathlib.Data.Nat.Factorization.Root, declared with their translated types ;)

(; doc: Ceiling division. If `a > 0`, then `b ⌈/⌉ a` is the least `c` such that `b ≤ a • c`. ;)
(; //LEAN:CeilDiv.ceilDiv ;)
CeilDiv__ceilDiv : (x7 : Elem x1) -> (x8 : Elem x) -> Elem x1.

(; doc: `Finsupp α M`, denoted `α →₀ M`, is the type of functions `f : α → M` such that `f x = 0` for all but finitely many `x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finsupp ;)
Finsupp : (x : Set) -> (M : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: `prod f g` is the product of `g a (f a)` over the support of `f`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finsupp.prod ;)
Finsupp__prod : (f : Elem (Finsupp x M)) -> (g : (Elem x -> Elem M -> N)) -> Elem N.

(; doc: Flooring division. If `a > 0`, then `b ⌊/⌋ a` is the greatest `c` such that `a • c ≤ b`. ;)
(; //LEAN:FloorDiv.floorDiv ;)
FloorDiv__floorDiv : (x7 : Elem x1) -> (x8 : Elem x) -> Elem x1.

(; doc: `n.factorization` is the finitely supported function `ℕ →₀ ℕ` mapping each prime factor of `n` to its multiplicity in `n`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Nat.factorization ;)
Nat__factorization : (n : Elem Nat) -> Elem (Finsupp Nat Nat).

(; doc: `if c then t else e` is notation for `ite c t e`, "if-then-else", which decides to return `t` or `e` depending on whether `c` is true or false. The explicit argument `c : Prop` does not have any actual computational content, but there is an additional `[Decidable c]` argument synthesized by typeclass inference which actually determines how to evaluate `c` to true or false. Write `if h : c then t else e` instead for a "dependent if-then-else" `dite`, which allows `t`/`e` to use the fact that `c` is true/false.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `if c then t else e` in identifiers is `ite` (use `left` for `t` and `right` for `e`). ;)
(; //LEAN:ite ;)
ite : (c : Prop) -> (t : Elem x) -> (e : Elem x) -> Elem x.

